<!--
  AI_Support_SOC — local detection content for IA1803
  Authored 2026-09-08 under DEC-056 (route B). Deployed to /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml.

  Four rules. One is a liveness beacon; three are detections that make the
  `ransomware`, `data_exfiltration` and `c2_beacon` categories of
  backend/app/ingest/category.py reachable on a host that has no network sensor,
  no web server and no anti-virus.

  Ids: 100999 is fixed by HEARTBEAT_RULE_ID (00-context-pack.md §6.3). The
  detections use a fresh 1003xx block: 100101, 100112, 100200, 100204 and 100205
  all appear in the 30-day archive and are deliberately not reused, so the
  archive's own history stays unambiguous.

  Signal basis, measured on this host on 2026-09-08 against the 30-day archive
  (92,030 alerts): auditd is live and the running audit ruleset carries an
  execve watch keyed "exec" (2,337 alerts) plus "privesc" (467), "modules" (5)
  and "identity" (4). All three detections below therefore hang off the stock
  `audit` group, the same construction the stock ruleset uses for 92600-92605.

  DEC-056 amendment 3 applies to rules 100301-100303: the behaviour the lab
  generates is real, but the detection logic that classifies it was written by
  the operator whose system is being evaluated. Each rule's premise, and the
  gap in that premise, is stated above it so a reader can judge the circularity
  rather than take the recall number at face value.
-->

<group name="local,">

  <!--
    100999 — pipeline heartbeat. Not a detection, and it is not pretending to be
    one: it is a liveness beacon for the ingest path (chot-v3-14-ngay.md D2).
    Its premise is thin by construction and that is the point — it must fire on
    a schedule with no adversary present.

    Level 3 is the minimum that enters alerts.json: ossec.conf line 25 sets
    log_alert_level to 3, and anything below it never reaches the indexer, so
    the P2-T11 freshness check would read zero forever.

    No `ignore` attribute on purpose. Rule 531, the stock sibling on this same
    parent, carries ignore="7200"; that would suppress repeat beats and make a
    live pipeline look dead to a 30-minute freshness window.

    Parent 530 ("Wazuh process monitoring rules", level 0) is the stock rule for
    `ossec: output: '<tag>': ...`, verified by logtest on this host.
  -->
  <rule id="100999" level="3">
    <if_sid>530</if_sid>
    <match>ossec: output: 'soc_heartbeat':</match>
    <description>SOC pipeline heartbeat.</description>
    <group>soc_heartbeat,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100301 — ransomware (T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact).

    Premise: on Linux the observable core of T1486 is bulk symmetric encryption
    of data in place. A ransomware operator that shells out to the platform's
    own crypto tools must supply the passphrase non-interactively, because there
    is no operator at a terminal to type it. An administrator encrypting one
    archive by hand is prompted instead. So the rule is not "openssl ran" — it
    is "a symmetric encryption tool ran with its passphrase supplied on the
    command line", which is a behavioural property, not a string.

    This is also what the T1486 Linux atomics in Atomic Red Team do, and
    chot-v3-14-ngay.md §C names Atomic Red Team as the scenario source.

    Where the premise is thin, stated: a family that implements AES in its own
    binary never execs a crypto tool and this rule will not see it — which is
    most modern Linux ransomware. It is a signal for the shell-script tier of
    the threat only. It also cannot see volume: one execve carries no count of
    files touched, so a developer encrypting a single backup with a passphrase
    flag is a false positive by design. The corroborating signal is FIM
    (rules 550/554) on the encrypted paths; that rule is not authored here
    because syscheck on this manager is scheduled at 12h and would not close
    the loop inside a lab window.

    Level 12 = critical in the pinned band (DEC-054).
  -->
  <rule id="100301" level="12">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">/(?:openssl|gpg|gpg2)$</field>
    <match type="pcre2">a\d+="enc".*a\d+="-(?:k|pass|kfile)"|a\d+="(?:-c|--symmetric)".*a\d+="--(?:batch|passphrase|passphrase-fd|passphrase-file)"</match>
    <description>Possible ransomware: non-interactive symmetric encryption via $(audit.exe).</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1486</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ransomware,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100302 — data exfiltration (T1041, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel).

    Premise: this host has no network sensor, so exfiltration is only visible at
    the point a process is told to send a local file outward. The rule requires
    that the command line names a local file as the body of an outbound request
    — curl's upload/PUT and multipart-from-file forms, wget's post-from-file
    form. Downloading (the overwhelmingly common use of both tools here) does
    not match, and neither does an API call whose body is inline text; the
    discriminator is a file path leaving the machine.

    Where the premise is thin, stated: T1041 proper means exfiltration over an
    already-established C2 channel, and a command-line upload is closer to T1048.
    T1041 is used because it is the id the resolver maps to `data_exfiltration`
    (category.py, tier 1) and because the alternative would be a category this
    estate cannot reach at all. The rule is also blind to the two exfiltration
    paths that matter most on a real estate — an interpreter opening its own
    socket, and a transfer inside an existing SSH session — because neither
    produces a distinguishable execve. The group `exfiltration` is carried as
    well so the alert still resolves at tier 3 if the MITRE block is ever lost
    in transport.

    Level 10 = high in the pinned band. Not critical: a single upload is a lead
    an analyst confirms, not a confirmed incident. It also stays below the
    level 11 threshold of the `<integration>` block at ossec.conf line 343.
  -->
  <rule id="100302" level="10">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.exe" type="pcre2">/(?:curl|wget)$</field>
    <match type="pcre2">a\d+="(?:-T|--upload-file)"|a\d+="--post-file|a\d+="(?:-F|--form|-d|--data|--data-binary|--data-raw)".*a\d+="[^"]*@/</match>
    <description>Possible data exfiltration: local file uploaded via $(audit.exe).</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1041</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>exfiltration,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    100303 — C2 channel (T1071, Application Layer Protocol).

    Premise: the two ways an operator attaches a shell to a socket on a stock
    Linux box without dropping a binary are bash's /dev/tcp and /dev/udp
    pseudo-devices, and netcat/socat's exec option. Neither has a routine
    administrative use on this host: /dev/tcp appears in no legitimate package
    script, and `nc -e` is compiled out of most distributions precisely because
    its only use is this one. Both are strong single-event signals.

    One property of auditd that this rule has to carry, found by running the
    command rather than by reasoning about it: auditd hex-encodes any execve
    argument that contains a space or a shell metacharacter. A reverse shell is
    always written `bash -c 'exec 3<>/dev/tcp/host/port'`, so the argument that
    names the pseudo-device is recorded as
    `a2=6578656320333C3E2F6465762F7463702F...`, never as a quoted string. The
    plain-text branch below therefore cannot fire on the case the rule exists
    for, and the hex branch is the one that does the work: 2F6465762F7463702F
    is `/dev/tcp/` and 2F6465762F7564702F is `/dev/udp/`. Hex is byte-aligned
    from the start of the argument, so a match on an odd nibble boundary would
    have to be a coincidence of the surrounding bytes; the plain branch is kept
    for the case where the path arrives as an argument of its own.

    Where the premise is thin, stated plainly: this rule does not detect
    beaconing. Beaconing is periodicity, and periodicity is not visible in one
    execve — it needs flow records this host does not produce (INVENTORY A8: the
    five stock Suricata rules decode as `json` and carry no MITRE, so installing
    Suricata would classify nothing). What this rule detects is the
    establishment of the channel. The category is named `c2_beacon` by
    category.py and the rule is mapped into it, but the evaluation chapter
    should say that `c2_beacon`'s recall on this estate is recall on channel
    setup, not on beacon traffic. An implant using plain HTTPS to a
    reputable-looking host is invisible here and no local rule can change that.

    Level 12 = critical in the pinned band.
  -->
  <rule id="100303" level="12">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <match type="pcre2">(?:a\d+=|proctitle=)[0-9A-F]*2F6465762F(?:7463|7564)702F|a\d+="[^"]*/dev/(?:tcp|udp)/[0-9a-zA-Z.:_-]+|exe="[^"]*/(?:nc|nc\.openbsd|nc\.traditional|ncat|netcat|socat)".*a\d+="(?:-e|--exec|--sh-exec|EXEC:[^"]*|SYSTEM:[^"]*)"</match>
    <description>Possible C2 channel: shell attached to a network socket.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>c2_beacon,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
